      },
      "lineNbr": 191,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-07-25T03:44:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is actually a pleonasm (assuming 32-bit value), because the most significant byte is always at the top, by definition. I don\u0027t think it\u0027s proper to refer to the first byte in an IPv4 address as \"most significant\" because an IPv4 address is not a scalar, it\u0027s 4 order scalars. You can just call it the first component of the address.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 191,
        "startChar": 47,
        "endLine": 191,
        "endChar": 85
      },
      "revId": "4248be404a018db04856031425809eac911b8e24",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "af080bb4_7d23556b",
        "filename": "core/java/android/net/NetworkUtils.java",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 191,
      "author": {
        "id": 1288034
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-07-25T08:40:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think the comments now consistently consider the address as a 32-bit scalar, which I find easier to document than 4 ordered 8-bit scalars (which would depend on some ordering convention), and consistent with RFC language.\nI changed the comment with an example and the same byte ordering note as the other similar methods - again let me know what you think.",
      "parentUuid": "45f9cf5f_09890c5b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 191,
        "startChar": 47,
        "endLine": 191,
        "endChar": 85
      },
      "revId": "4248be404a018db04856031425809eac911b8e24",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
